~2–3 hours

DAY 3
Q41–Q50
~2 hours
+ revision

That gives you time to actually understand why the solution works.

And don't skip the senior topics: groupingBy, downstream collectors, toMap merge functions, reduce vs collect, and parallel streams are particularly important because they expose whether you really understand Streams rather than just knowing map() and filter(). Oracle's documentation also notes that parallel streams are not automatically faster and require care around ordering, side effects, and collection strategy.

My recommended target for you
50 questions
    ↓
~30 easy/medium
    ↓
~15 advanced
    ↓
~5 senior/tricky

Don't try to finish 50 just to say "done." A much better goal is to be able to take a new Stream problem in an interview and identify the pattern within 30–60 seconds.

For your next step, start with Q1–Q10 only. Once those are comfortable, move to map/filter/flatMap, then Collectors, then groupingBy.

Absolutely. This is the best way for you to learn Streams: one problem at a time, understand the thought process, solve it yourself, then compare with the solution.

We'll follow the 50-question roadmap, but I won't dump all 50 solutions at once.

Question 1 — Find Even Numbers

Given:

List<Integer> numbers = List.of(10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35, 40);
Requirement

Using Java Streams, find all even numbers.

Expected output:

[10, 20, 30, 40]
First think before coding

Ask yourself:

"I have a list of numbers. I only want elements that satisfy a condition."

Which Stream operation should you think of?

filter()

filter() keeps elements that match a given condition and produces another Stream. Oracle's Stream documentation describes this as a common pipeline operation.

Solution
import java.util.List;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        List<Integer> numbers =
                List.of(10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35, 40);

        List<Integer> evenNumbers = numbers.stream()
                .filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)
                .toList();

        System.out.println(evenNumbers);
    }
}

Output:

[10, 20, 30, 40]
Understand the pipeline

Don't memorize the code. Understand this:

numbers
   ↓
stream()
   ↓
filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)
   ↓
10 20 30 40
   ↓
toList()

The important line is:

.filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)

For every number:

10 → 10 % 2 = 0 → KEEP
15 → 15 % 2 = 1 → REMOVE
20 → 20 % 2 = 0 → KEEP
25 → 25 % 2 = 1 → REMOVE
Interview question

Why don't we use map() here?

Because we aren't transforming each element into another value. We're selecting/removing elements based on a condition.

filter() → SELECT
map()    → TRANSFORM

This distinction is extremely important for Streams interviews.

Your turn

Don't look back at the solution. Write Q1 yourself:

List<Integer> numbers = List.of(10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35, 40);

// write your solution

Send me your code. I'll review it like an interviewer, then we'll move to Question 2.

next question
Question 2 — Find numbers greater than 20

Given:

List<Integer> numbers =
        List.of(10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35, 40);
Requirement

Using Java Streams, find all numbers greater than 20.

Expected output:

[25, 30, 35, 40]
Think first

Again, we're selecting elements based on a condition, so the main operation is:

filter()

A Stream pipeline can use filter() to keep only elements that satisfy its predicate.

Solution
List<Integer> result = numbers.stream()
        .filter(n -> n > 20)
        .toList();

System.out.println(result);

Output:

[25, 30, 35, 40]
Understand it
10  → 10 > 20 → false → remove
15  → 15 > 20 → false → remove
20  → 20 > 20 → false → remove
25  → 25 > 20 → true  → keep
30  → 30 > 20 → true  → keep
35  → 35 > 20 → true  → keep
40  → 40 > 20 → true  → keep
Interview point

Notice the difference:

.filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)

means:

Keep numbers satisfying even condition.

.filter(n -> n > 20)

means:

Keep numbers satisfying greater-than-20 condition.

So the important concept is:

filter() = select elements based on a condition.

Small challenge

Can you modify it to find:

[25, 30, 35]

That means greater than 20 AND less than 40.

Try it mentally first. The solution is:

List<Integer> result = numbers.stream()
        .filter(n -> n > 20 && n < 40)
        .toList();

Next question will introduce map(), so you'll start seeing the important difference between filter = select and map = transform.

next problem
Question 3 — Convert Strings to Uppercase

This is our first map() problem.

Given:

List<String> names =
        List.of("ravi", "ram", "anil", "suresh");
Requirement

Using Java Streams, convert every name to uppercase.

Expected output:

[RAVI, RAM, ANIL, SURESH]
Think first

In the previous questions, we used:

filter()

because we wanted to select some elements.

Here, we want to change/transform each element:

ravi   → RAVI
ram    → RAM
anil   → ANIL
suresh → SURESH

So the Stream operation is:

map()

map() applies a function to each element and produces a stream containing the resulting values. It is an intermediate operation.

Solution
List<String> result = names.stream()
        .map(String::toUpperCase)
        .toList();

System.out.println(result);

Output:

[RAVI, RAM, ANIL, SURESH]

You can also write the lambda version:

List<String> result = names.stream()
        .map(name -> name.toUpperCase())
        .toList();
The important difference

Remember this:

filter() → SELECT
map()    → TRANSFORM

For example:

.filter(name -> name.length() > 4)

means:

Keep only some names.

Whereas:

.map(name -> name.toUpperCase())

means:

Transform every name.

Interview memory trick
FILTER = Which elements do I want?
MAP    = What should each element become?
Your small challenge

Without looking at the solution, write a Stream that converts:

List<Integer> numbers =
        List.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);

into:

[2, 4, 6, 8, 10]

using map().

Then we'll go to Question 4.